  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Near-critical to critical fire weather peaks this afternoon and\n   evening from gusty winds and exceptionally dry conditions.\n\n-  Widespread smoke continues to degrade air quality and\n   visibility driven by variable upstream fire activity in WA, OR,\n   and BC. \n\n-  Wednesday wind shift in SW MT enhances fire weather concerns\n   potentially driving active fires toward new fuel sources.\n\nA subtle upper-level wave and weak frontal passage will further\nenhance critical fire weather conditions this afternoon across the\nregion. Surface wind gusts up to 30 mph are expected, \nparticularly across higher terrain. These winds combined with \nexceptionally dry fuels, warm temperatures, and continuously poor\novernight recoveries off valley floors will sustain critical \nthresholds through the late evening hours. Thermal belts will \ncontinue to influence poor overnight recoveries though \nincreasingly active flow aloft over the next several days will \ncontinue to slowly tamper the strength of these stable inversion \nenvironments. \n\nWidespread wildfire smoke will continue across the Northern\nRockies, with surface impacts dependent on upstream plume\ntransport from regional wildfires across the Pacific Northwest and\nthe timing of these plumes entering the region. In the near-\nterm, very dense smoke is anticipated in NW Montana on Sunday.\n\nA gradual moistening and slight cooling trend is expected next\nweek. However, this moderating airmass is unlikely to \nsignificantly suppress ongoing local and regional wildfire \nactivity. Light to moderate winds will persist. Furthermore, a \nweak upper level disturbance on Monday will increase wind speeds \nand gust potential out of the west once again especially in \nSouthwest Montana. A shallow low-level north and northeasterly \npush is anticipated for Wednesday creating a distinct shift in \nwind direction which could be problematic for certain slope \nalignments and fuel availability along active flanks of ongoing \nfire activity in Southwest Montana and areas near the Continental \nDivide.\n\nThere is modest confidence for some notable fire suppression due \nto precipitation chances and colder temperatures inbound with the\nmid-late week trough. The best chances for cooler and wetter\nconditions remains confined to areas north and along the Canadian\nBorder into the Flathead National Forest closer to the cold core \nwhere higher moisture availability and lifting mechanisms of the \nforecast trough are expected. The time period for this weather\nsystem would be Wednesday into Thursday though model agreement in\nexact impacts and timing of this weather system are still not \ntotally locked-in.",
